WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.418
How do you know a relationship really works? You test it.

2
00:00:04.418 --> 00:00:09.022
In this lesson, you will learn to build quick test visuals from two tables,

3
00:00:09.022 --> 00:00:14.284
click a value to watch the filter move, and fix the relationship when it does not.

4
00:00:14.284 --> 00:00:15.600
Let's test our model!

5
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:20.950
On a new page, I start with a bar chart:

6
00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:24.400
employee IDs by ship address, from the North Customers table.

7
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.712
In the Format pane, I can adjust the axis, the values, and the bars.

8
00:00:41.712 --> 00:00:46.760
But for testing, a simple table is clearer, so I switch to a table visual.

9
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.963
Now two table visuals side by side:

10
00:01:04.963 --> 00:01:09.620
ship addresses from North Customers, and customer IDs from another table.

11
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.647
Click an address in the first table.

12
00:01:39.647 --> 00:01:41.537
Does the second table change?

13
00:01:41.537 --> 00:01:43.806
If it does, the relationship works.

14
00:01:43.806 --> 00:01:49.100
If nothing changes, the tables are not connected, or the filter direction is wrong.

15
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.084
Here I add customer IDs from South Customers.

16
00:02:10.084 --> 00:02:12.782
Clicking an address does not filter them.

17
00:02:12.782 --> 00:02:15.480
So there is no working relationship yet.

18
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:43.980
In Model view, I create a New relationship between All Customers and South Customers on Customer ID.

19
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.113
Power BI sets it to many to one, with a single filter direction.

20
00:03:01.113 --> 00:03:03.960
I check that it is active, and click Save.

21
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.271
Back on the report, I test again.

22
00:03:11.271 --> 00:03:12.569
Build, click, check, fix.

23
00:03:12.569 --> 00:03:14.840
That is how you trust your model!

